# SESSION 1 — 2026-07-24 (BLOCKED, archived below)

Session 1 could not start: the venv was stale (vLLM 0.19.1 / torch 2.10) relative
to the branch, so vLLM engine-core init raised `ImportError: cannot import name
'CompilationTimes'`. No baseline was obtained. The env has since been rebuilt
(see SESSION 2, Step 0 now PASS). Session 1's prep notes (model param names, the
bfp4 override file, the CI-wheel-uplift analysis) are preserved in git history and
summarized above under Model facts. Key artifact retained:
`mixed_precision/overrides_qwen05b_mlp_bfp4.json`.

===============================================================================
SESSION 2b — FULL-PRECISION + CHISEL INVESTIGATION (2026-07-27)
Trigger: baseline TOP1 p5 (68.75%) < 90% -> investigate whether MP is the cause.
===============================================================================

Step A — full-precision (bf16 weights + bf16 kv + act off) e2e, vLLM:
  TOP1 p5=68.75% median=69.53% mean=67.82% min=14.06%
  TOP5 p5=90.62% median=91.41% min=32.81%
  => IDENTICAL TOP1 p5 to the bfp8-weight baseline (68.75%). One user still
     collapses to 14% even in full precision. CONCLUSION: mixed precision is NOT
     the cause of the low accuracy; the ~69% ceiling is inherent to the model /
     teacher-forced-vs-CPU reference (0.5B model + one outlier user).

Env: had to rebuild plugin with `--build-type explorer` (=> TTXLA_ENABLE_EXPLORER
  => TT_RUNTIME_DEBUG=ON + python bindings + chisel/golden/ttmlir pkgs) for chisel.
  chisel smoke (tests/torch/ops/test_mul.py) produced numerics records -> chain OK.

Steps B/C — one decoder layer via test_llms.py::test_qwen_2_5_0_5b (torch-xla,
  IN-PROCESS so chisel captures; vLLM runs model in a worker subprocess and would
  not be captured), --num-layers 1 --enable-chisel:
  * B full precision (weight="") and C baseline (weight="bfp_bf8"); kv=None both.
  * GOTCHA: chisel needs trace_enabled=False. With trace on, the first attempt
    HUNG ~2h on "TT_FATAL: Reads are not supported during trace capture".
  * Each captured 283 numerics records (accumulated mode only; isolated mode had
    0 pcc records via this fixture).
  * B vs C: 283/283 records IDENTICAL pcc. Reason: chisel did NOT score any
    ttnn.matmul/ttnn.linear (254 goldens evicted; matmuls absent from numerics).
    The scored ops (slice_static, pad, fill_cache, typecast, rms_norm, sdpa,
    elementwise) are weight-dtype-invariant -> identical across fp/bfp8.
  * Worst scored pcc (both runs): ttnn.max / ttnn.eq / ttnn.argmax (=0, DEGENERATE
    on argmax/boolean outputs) and ttnn.fill_cache (0.17-0.47, likely a chisel
    in-place-cache accounting artifact). No golden-backed matmul/norm showed a
    real bf16 fault.

METHODOLOGY LEARNINGS (candidate skill edits, pending user approval):
  1. When baseline TOP1 < 90%, run the full-precision investigation FIRST
     (bf16 e2e) to decide if MP is even the culprit before chisel/op work.
  2. chisel requires: (a) `--build-type explorer` build (TT_RUNTIME_DEBUG +
     bindings + chisel/golden pkgs); (b) trace_enabled=False (reads unsupported
     during trace capture -> hang); (c) an IN-PROCESS path (test_llms.py, NOT
     vLLM workers). Run one layer with --num-layers 1 (works in test_llms.py;
     ignored by the vLLM benchmark test).
  3. chisel's golden is promoted from device tensors, so it measures KERNEL
     correctness (device vs torch-of-its-inputs), NOT quantization-vs-fp32; and
     in this run it did not score matmul/linear (evicted). => chisel is good for
     catching a broken/regressing kernel op, but does NOT by itself attribute
     e2e accuracy loss to weight quantization. Ignore degenerate max/eq/argmax.
  4. For "is MP the cause?" the decisive signal was the e2e full-precision A/B,
     not chisel.
